    "code_released": "https://github.com/DensoITLab/PoF-v1",
    "peer_reviewed": "International Conference on Machine Learning, PMLR 162, 2022",
    "problems": "The paper reports test error rather than training-test gaps for its dataset comparisons. The Hessian-component comparison is presented in figures, with only a qualitative description and rough factor-of-two summary in the text. The classifier fine-tuning result is from one training instance. Some conclusions about improvement are stated as averages, but the table supplies per-checkpoint errors.",
    "rows_left_out": "No results were left out. The paper's Figure 1 toy MLP and Figure 2 mini-batch statistics are setup/method motivation rather than reported outcomes directly bearing on the listed versions; cited prior-study results are excluded.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "Random-label fitting, natural-label held-out accuracy, and chance-level held-out performance with random labels."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "Width-dependent test error beyond the width required for zero training error."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "Convolutional versus fully connected networks and the contribution of local connectivity or initialization."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "Adaptive versus SGD optimizer performance under limited or broader tuning and recovery strategies."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "Batch-size comparisons under fixed epoch budgets and adaptations that remove large-batch penalties."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "Dropout effects, training-set-size dependence, underfitting, and removal alongside weight decay."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "Magnitude-scaled weight-perturbation tolerance, its association with train-test gaps at comparable training loss, and ranking against norm and margin measures. Classifier-space Hessian estimates and test error do not establish these."
      },
      {
        "version": "V8",
        "parts": "Removing highly memorized examples versus equally many random examples and retraining."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "The study uses a broad flatness premise to design PoF, but does not operationalize the specific V7 claim. SAM is not an adaptive optimizer comparison of the kind specified in V4. Different batch sizes within PoF's algorithm do not constitute a V5 batch-size experiment. No omitted result in the supplied text warrants a verdict on a listed version."
